992 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 515 0 R
/E (This article explains the phenomenon of superconductivity as well as fundamental properties of superconductors. It examines the quantum mechanical mechanisms that enable electrical current to flow within the superconductor without resistance, as well as the expulsion of magnetic fields, by a superconducting material, known as the Meissner effect. In addition the article outlines the history of invention and improvement of superconducting materials, and their real world applications.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
993 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 515 0 R
/E (Metals are widely used as electrical conductors due to their ability to allow the flow of electric current with relatively low resistance. The conductivity of the material is inversely proportional to its resistivity. However, there are certain materials that allow current to flow without energy loss, resulting in effectively zero electrical resistance. These materials are known as superconductors. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
994 0 obj
<<
