1004 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 516 0 R
/E (This coherent quantum state allows electrons to move through the material without scattering, eliminating electrical resistance. Unlike in normal conductors, where electrons behave as independent particles, superconducting electrons act collectively, preventing energy dissipation. As a result, the wavefunction they abide by is robust to the scattering from the atoms or imperfections in the substance, effectively creating a current with 0 resistance. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 516 0 R
/E (This explains why in conventional conductors resistivity gradually decreases with the decrease of the temperature, whereas in superconductors it drops abruptly to zero at the transition temperature \(Figure 1.2\))
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
