1016 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 516 0 R
/E (As stated above superconductors do not have resistance thus once there is a current in the circuit made entirely of a superconducting material it theoretically will never diminish unless the external force acts upon it. Once the superconductor is introduced to a magnetic field, persistent surface currents are induced within the material - eddy currents \(a localized electric current induced in a conductor by a varying magnetic field\) \(Figure 2.1\). Since such currents are not affected by resistance within the superconductor their magnitude is directly proportional to the strength of the magnetic field to which they are exposed. However, those currents also produce their own magnetic field. This field adjusts in response to the applied magnetic field, opposing and cancelling it within the interior of the superconductor. This means that the magnetic field produced by the current in the superconductor is equal in magnitude and mirrored \(opposite\) in direction to the external magnetic field that acts upon the superconductor, effectively counteracting it. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
1017 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 516 0 R
/E (Moreover, unlike a conventional conductor, a superconductor will expel a magnetic field even if that field is already present when the material is cooled below its critical temperature. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
