1028 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 517 0 R
/E (Their previously unseen properties have puzzled physicists all around the world, and many explanations of the phenomena were proposed with little to no success. Until in 1935, the theory proposed by Fritz and Heinz London \(known as the London theory\) was the first success in the field, which was followed by another theory put forward by Vitaly Ginzburg and Lev Landau in the 1950s.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1029 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 517 0 R
/E (Though the proposed theory was satisfactory it was not perfect and soon a more complete explanation emerged with the work of John Bardeen, Leon Cooper and John Robert Schrieffer. It was nicknamed the BCS theory in their honour. This theory successfully explained superconductivity in conventional materials through the formation of Cooper pairs and remains fundamental to the field. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1030 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 517 0 R
/E (This demonstrates that the effect is not simply due to electromagnetic induction, but is instead a fundamental property of the superconducting state.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
