/P 523 0 R
/E (Abstract:)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 523 0 R
/E (These findings underline the practical value of hybrid configurations as an adaptable framework for improving the reliability, cost-effectiveness, and environmental performance of renewable power generation across a wide range of geographical and economic contexts.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 523 0 R
/E (Energy demand is rising. Whether driven by growth in the world's population or by industrial development, the need for greater energy supply grows with every passing moment. The moral imperative to reduce the rapid depletion of natural resources and the environmental damage caused by the use of gas and crude oil directs attention towards renewable energy. These sources, while addressing environmental damage and carbon footprints, pose a major challenge: unreliability [1]. Sources such as solar or wind suffer from significant reliability issues due to meteorological conditions, while others, such as biomass or geothermal, face reliability issues that depend on location. The issue of unreliability can be addressed with Hybrid Renewable Energy Systems \(HRES\) [2]. By combining two or more energy sources, the weaknesses of individual systems can be compensated for, increasing overall reliability.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 523 0 R
/E (The literature also shows a gradual shift in focus, moving away from systems that pair renewable sources with non-renewable backup generation towards configurations built entirely from renewable sources. This shift reflects the broader push towards full decarbonisation, and it is a theme this review returns to in later sections when considering the long-term direction of HRES design.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 523 0 R
/E (A Review of Hybrid Renewable Energy Systems: Modelling and Integration)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
