1158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 524 0 R
/E (Due to this disadvantage, PV systems can be less reliable as standalone power sources. Paired with other energy sources, PV systems prove to be not only viable but also an influential contributor to clean, environmentally friendly energy generation [3], as illustrated in Fig. 1.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 524 0 R
/E (Another widely used renewable energy source is wind generation. Wind turbines convert the kinetic energy of moving air into mechanical power, which is then converted to electricity by a generator. When wind passes the turbines, it creates lift, causing the blades to rotate. This rotation powers the generator, producing electricity without the need for combustion or harmful emissions. Wind power is widely considered a clean alternative to energy generation and can be implemented in large-scale wind farms as well as smaller setups, whether grid-connected or not. Wind turbines, however, are not without their own challenges. Their energy output is heavily dependent on wind speed and wind direction, which can be influenced by changes in weather conditions, seasonal changes, and geographical location. These issues may hinder the reliability of this energy source when used alone. However, combined with other renewable or conventional energy sources, wind generation becomes a highly effective contributor to sustainable power production [4], [5], as shown in Fig. 2. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 524 0 R
/E (Hydropower is one of the oldest and most established forms of renewable energy, using the natural flow of water to produce electricity. In a typical system, flowing water drives a turbine, which rotates and moves a generator to create electricity. Because the water cycle is continuous and replenishes itself with rainfall and evaporation, hydropower can provide a stable, large-scale energy supply with minimal loss and minimal emissions. Unlike solar and wind, which suffer from intermittency due to the nature of their power production, hydroelectric power plants are often capable of providing a consistent power output. This energy source relies mainly on the availability of water resources, meaning that geographical location and seasonal patterns play a significant role in performance. Additionally, large hydropower plants can raise environmental concerns including habitat disruption and impacts on ecosystems. Use of these systems in hybrid renewable energy systems can enhance efficiency, as well as energy security [6], [7], as shown in Fig. 3. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1161 0 obj
<<
