1184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 526 0 R
/E (Bae and Kwasinski [11] present a simulation of a 30 kW hybrid microgrid comprising wind and solar systems, along with a separate energy storage system. The wind power is modelled using a permanent magnet synchronous generator, which produces electricity without needing extra parts like gearboxes. The solar panels are modelled to respond to changes in sunlight. Both sources, along with the load, are tested under real-world conditions where wind, sun and demand continuously change. To manage this, the system uses a special DC-DC converter that can take in power from multiple sources and connect them to one common DC bus. A smart controller \(MPPT\) was implemented and simulated for each source to ensure efficient use of the system's power output. The simulation shows that this system stays stable and efficient even when conditions vary. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 526 0 R
/E (Erdinc and Uzunoglu [9] discuss various modelling and sizing methods for hybrid renewable energy \(HRE\) systems. The combination of different hybrid renewable energy sources with backup units is stated to offer a more economical, environmentally friendly, and reliable power supply compared to single renewable source systems. The key challenge discussed is optimally sizing the components to meet load requirements while still keeping costs low. Different sizing approaches are proposed, such as using software like HOMER to optimally size and simulate HRE systems, or using Genetic Algorithms to account for meteorological changes and fluctuations, and the ways they impact the system. HOMER works by simulating different combinations of power sources, storage, and loads, then comparing them to find the most cost-effective and reliable hybrid energy system design, as shown in Fig. 5.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 526 0 R
/E (Because the converter combines the system's different energy outputs in a single stage rather than running them through separate stages, the system reduces energy losses and costs. Overall, the results suggest that this system is reliable and affordable as an option for microgrids, especially those with constantly shifting power supply and demand.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
