/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2 4 3]
>>
endobj
1206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 528 0 R
/E (On the other side, renewable distributed generation uses sources such as photovoltaic panels, wind turbines, or small hydropower systems. These offer clean, sustainable energy while facing intermittency issues. To address this variability, storage solutions such as batteries or flywheels are often integrated into these distributed systems, ensuring a continuous energy output. With the combination of these elements, distributed generation systems enhance energy security and lower transmission losses due to their proximity to demand, all while contributing towards more sustainable energy generation [19], as shown in Fig. 7.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 528 0 R
/E (Ye et al. [20] presented a microgrid architecture combining wind turbine generators, photovoltaic panels, and hydro generators, and built a detailed simulation model using EMTP/ATP software \(software used to test components or machines under real-world scenarios, such as simulating changes in voltage and/or current that occur in power systems when something disturbs the system\) to test how all parts interact. In their model, each power source is represented with its own electronic interface \(for example, converters or inverters\) and control systems so that the full dynamic behaviour \(how voltages, currents, and power change over time\) can be captured. The control strategies implemented used active power/frequency droop control to manage how much real power each source contributes and to stabilise frequency. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 528 0 R
/E (Figure 7: Classification of distributed generation technologies into renewable, non-renewable and storage sources)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1209 0 obj
<<
