/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1 3 2]
>>
endobj
1216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (Calculating the data over a 20-year lifetime, the PV system alone produced approximately 16,378 kWh compared to 2,617.8 kWh for wind under a 1 kW baseline. When including economic factors such as installation and maintenance costs, the PV system was determined to have the lowest cost per kilowatt-hour at $0.47, followed by the hybrid system at $0.51, while wind alone reached $3.21. Based on these findings, the authors conclude that PV is the most efficient and profitable option for the studied site, with hybrid systems also showing promise.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (Wind and solar energy are widely used renewable energy sources, but face challenges due to intermittency caused by changes in weather or seasonal variations. Ahmed [22] proposes the integration of fuel cells to address these limitations, providing more reliable, continuous power generation. In this system, photovoltaic modules and wind turbines act as the primary sources, connected to DC-DC converters equipped with maximum power point tracking \(MPPT\) to maximise power output. This combined output is then fed into a common DC bus and linked through an inverter, while the fuel cell subsystem is activated to supply the system when the renewable sources cannot meet demand. Simulation results demonstrate that this system, and in particular the use of fuel cells, effectively suppresses fluctuations caused by wind speed or solar irradiance.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (Photovoltaic \(PV\) paired with diesel hybrid systems are among the simplest and most widely applied hybrid renewable energy systems, especially in rural areas with limited grid access. These systems consist of PV modules that generate electricity during daylight, while a diesel generator provides power during low-irradiance periods, where the PV system is underperforming. Yamegueu et al. [23] investigated a PV-diesel system without an energy storage system, under constant load, to assess its reliability and efficiency in off-grid applications. Their study demonstrated that the system performs efficiently under high solar radiation and heavier load periods, with the diesel generator sized to meet peak demand and maintain a stable energy output. A key advantage of this system is its reduced fossil fuel consumption compared to standalone diesel generators. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (This not only lowers operational costs but also facilitates the transition to more renewable-energy-focused power generation, while meeting demand and lowering emissions. The absence of an energy storage system, however, has its own drawbacks, including a heavier reliance on diesel during prolonged periods of low solar irradiance, which also raises costs and environmental impact.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (IV. Discussion)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (The findings from the reviewed literature on hybrid renewable energy systems \(HRES\) highlight several themes: reliability improvement, cost effectiveness, and sustainability. While standalone renewable systems such as solar, wind or hydro each present environmental advantages over fossil fuel-based generation, their individual limitations, primarily intermittency and location dependence, make them less reliable for a continuous energy supply. Hybrid systems, by combining multiple energy sources with complementary characteristics, have emerged as a practical pathway to achieving both efficiency and reliability while advancing global sustainability goals.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (One key point is the role of complementary resource behaviour in reducing intermittency. For example, the integration of solar and wind has shown strong promise, as both peak at different times. While solar irradiance is more abundant during daytime, allowing peak PV system output, wind generation can increase during evenings, nights and seasons when solar irradiance is low, allowing these two systems to almost perfectly complement each other. The Lebanese case study [21] confirmed that this hybrid system can mitigate seasonal variability and balance the output between the two sources. This balance, however, does not entirely eliminate intermittency, which underlines the importance of incorporating storage technologies and units such as fuel cells or diesel generators.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (Fuel cells have been repeatedly emphasised as a reliable backup option. Their rapid response in delivering a stable voltage makes them effective at smoothing out fluctuations from variable renewable sources. Simulations demonstrated that systems such as a wind-solar-fuel cell combination could suppress unpredictability while maintaining stable power output. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 529 0 R
/E (20)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
