/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1 3 2]
>>
endobj
1228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (Even though the results showed a high upfront cost for fuel cells and hydrogen storage systems, their contribution to these systems and the reliability they provide make them an attractive option, especially for grid-connected systems or situations where an uninterrupted supply is essential.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (V. Conclusion)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (Another point raised by the literature is the challenge of optimal sizing and system design. Improper sizing can erode the efficiency and benefits of HRES, leading to energy shortages or unnecessary investment in oversized components. Various approaches, ranging from simulation software such as HOMER to advanced algorithms such as Particle Swarm Optimisation \(PSO\) and Genetic Algorithms, have been proposed to account for load profiles, meteorological variability, and cost estimation. These methods are important for ensuring that hybrid systems deliver both technical reliability and economic viability.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (Environmental and economic trade-offs are another point of discussion. While systems like solar and biomass hybrids can achieve a lower levelised cost of energy \(LCOE\) compared to solar and diesel systems, the availability of biomass resources is region-specific, limiting their universal application. Hydropower, though reliable, raises ecological concerns such as habitat disruption. Thus, the selection of hybrid configurations must be context-specific, considering not only technical and economic factors but also social and environmental impacts.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (The transition towards renewable energy is no longer a choice but a necessity in order to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, slow global warming, and preserve natural resources for future generations. While individual renewable systems such as solar, wind, or hydro offer significant environmental advantages, their dependence on meteorological and geographic factors limits their reliability when used alone. The literature reviewed shows that hybrid renewable energy systems offer an effective solution by combining the complementary strengths of multiple resources into a single integrated setup.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (Studies have shown that pairing resources such as solar and wind can reduce intermittency. At the same time, the use of fuel cells or diesel units can ensure a continuous supply even during periods of low renewable output. Simulation tools and optimisation algorithms have also helped improve system design, reduce energy losses, and ensure cost effectiveness. In addition, real-world experiments of such systems confirm the practicality of these setups and their ability to deliver reliable power over extended periods.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (However, hybrid systems are not without limitations. The absence or high cost of storage technologies can increase reliance on non-renewable backup sources, while economic feasibility remains highly dependent on local resource availability and regional conditions. Environmental and social considerations also remain important when evaluating specific resources such as hydropower or biomass.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/E (Overall, the reviewed studies suggest that hybrid renewable energy systems are not a one-size-fits-all solution but a flexible framework adaptable to diverse geographical, economic, and social contexts. For developed regions with strong grid infrastructure, the focus lies on optimising renewable energy systems with advanced control and storage integration. In contrast, developing regions and remote areas may continue to rely on transitional systems such as solar-diesel, gradually moving towards cleaner alternatives as technology becomes more affordable.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
