1282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (Ocular immune privilege is a highly regulated state in which standard immune responses are modulated to preserve vision as a primary function of the eye and protect intraocular structures from inflammation-induced damage. It is maintained via blood-ocular barriers, immunosuppressive microenvironments and systemic immune tolerance. Despite such autonomy, the protection is not absolute. Ocular trauma or surgery may lead to the disruption of immune privilege, which then can contribute to sympathetic ophthalmia, a bilateral granulomatous inflammatory disorder. This provides a valuable model for understanding major consequences of tissue-specific immune regulation breakdown with further implications for the field of transplantology. In this review, cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying ocular immune privilege and standard immune response are examined, with particular emphasis on normal physiology followed by a pathophysiological perspective. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (Abstract:)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (The eye is a naturally occurring example of such. Great reliance on highly specialised structures responsible for vision creates a strong physiological requirement for mechanisms that can limit potentially destructive immune activity without compromising immune defence. Immune privilege introduces a concept of selectivity and, therefore, addresses an important matter as to how an organ with low regenerative ability may retain its capacity for an immune response whilst protected from immune-mediated damage. Understanding the mechanisms underlying this balance, as well as the consequences of its disruption, provides a broader insight into the regulation of immune responses in the body and informs the development of strategies to mitigate immune-mediated rejection in transplantation and promote immune tolerance. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (The immune system is a complex group of defence responses found in humans and other advanced vertebrates that helps repel disease-causing organisms called pathogens1. Normally, its duty would include not only allocating a response to an invading pathogen, but also distinguishing self from non-self. To do that, both innate and adaptive mechanisms would be used.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (Ocular Immune Privilege: Mechanisms and Pathophysiological Consequences)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
