/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Antimicrobial resistance \(AMR\) occurs when microorganisms that cause infection no longer respond to antimicrobial medicines. This resistance develops naturally over time as pathogens evolve through genetic changes. Antibiotics have been among the greatest advances in modern medicine, greatly reducing illness and death from infectious diseases. However, antibiotic resistance is now a major challenge, leading to increased illness and mortality and posing a serious global health risk. The World Health Organisation \(WHO\) considers AMR one of the top public health and development threats worldwide.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (This article examines the scientific aetiology of antibiotic resistance, providing a comprehensive overview of its development and its implications as a preeminent global health crisis.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Abstract:)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Antibiotics have been a foundation of modern medicine, acting as vital antibacterial agents that treat infections by controlling bacterial growth and reproduction. This medical revolution has saved millions of lives by competently addressing previously life-threatening bacterial infections. )
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (However, the misuse and excessive use of antibiotics, combined with incomplete treatment and inadequate hygiene, have precipitated a worldwide health crisis. )
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Antimicrobial resistance \(AMR\) is the process by which microorganisms that cause infections become resistant to antimicrobial drugs. AMR is a natural process that develops over time through genetic changes in pathogens.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
