>>
endobj
1396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (As a specific form of antimicrobial resistance \(AMR\), antibiotic resistance occurs when antibiotic use creates selective pressure, favouring bacteria with genetic variations that enable them to survive exposure to antibacterial agents. Consequently, antibiotics become ineffective, producing prolonged infections, increased therapeutic complexity, and a substantial risk of fatality.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Furthermore, the marked increase in AMR among common bacterial pathogens now threatens treatment efficacy and jeopardises successful outcomes for critically ill patients. Significantly, the World Health Organisation has named antibiotic resistance as one of the three most significant public health threats of the 21st century. )
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
1399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (II. Genetic Basis of Antibiotic Resistance)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
1400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 540 0 R
/E (Bacteria are genetically resilient to environmental threats. Driven by the misuse and overuse of antibiotics, bacteria develop resistance through major genetic strategies. These can be organism-specific or acquired through spontaneous mutation or horizontal gene transfer. Bacteria can also temporarily exhibit adaptive resistance in response to external stimuli.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
