<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (Intrinsic resistance is the natural resistance of microbial pathogens to certain antibiotics due to structural or biochemical traits. In contrast to acquired resistance, it is an inherited trait that functions as a natural defence mechanism \(Nimmana and Nguyen, 2026\).)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (2.2 Adaptive Resistance)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (Adaptive resistance refers to transient resistance developed in response to external stresses, including efflux pump expression \(transport proteins that expel substances, including some antibiotics\) or biofilm formation \(Nimmana and Nguyen, 2026\). Adaptive resistance is a temporary alteration in bacterial gene expression or physiology in response to environmental conditions, rather than a permanent change in genetic material.)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (2.3 Acquired Resistance)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [8]
>>
