/E (2.3.1 Genetic Mutations)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (Spontaneous point mutations may alter antibiotic target sites, reduce drug binding affinity, or decrease cellular permeability, causing antibiotic resistance. )
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (2.3.2 Horizontal Gene Transfer \(HGT\))
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (HGT is the movement of genetic information between organisms; it includes the spread of antibiotic resistance genes among bacteria, fueling pathogen evolution \(Alita R., 2015\). This process occurs through three primary mechanisms: conjugation, transformation, and transduction \(Alita B. 2015\). )
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (3. Mechanistic Basis of Antibiotic Resistance)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E ( Bacteria have developed refined mechanisms over millions of years to evade antimicrobial molecules. Resistance is often achieved through multiple simultaneous biochemical pathways, allowing the cell to survive the antibiotic's effects. The primary mechanisms include:)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 541 0 R
/T (3.1 Modification or Destruction of the Antibiotic)
/E (3.1 Modification or Destruction of the Antibiotic)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
1419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 541 0 R
/E (This occurs through enzymatic degradation of the molecule or the addition of specific chemical moieties, both of which prevent the drug from engaging its molecular target. One of the primary resistance strategies involves inactivating antibiotics through enzymatic processes.)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
