1431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 542 0 R
/E (To be effective, antibiotics must cross the bacterial membranes of bacteria \(particularly in Gram-negative species\). Bacteria reduce antimicrobial uptake by limiting entry through the outer or cytoplasmic membranes, preventing the drug from reaching intracellular targets \(Munita and Arias, 2016\).)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 542 0 R
/T (3.3 Alterations in Target Sites)
/E (3.3 Alterations in Target Sites)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 542 0 R
/T (3.4 Resistance Through Global Cellular Adaptations)
/E (3.4 Resistance Through Global Cellular Adaptations)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 542 0 R
/E (Over evolutionary timescales, bacteria have evolved sophisticated adaptive mechanisms to withstand hostile environments, such as those within the human host. Bacteria compete for nutrients, evade antagonistic molecules from rival microorganisms, and withstand host immune defences to establish themselves in specific niches. Thus, bacterial pathogens employ complex strategies that protect essential cellular processes, including cell wall biosynthesis and membrane homeostasis.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 542 0 R
/T (3.2.2 Efflux Pumps)
/E (3.2.2 Efflux Pumps)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 542 0 R
/E (Efflux pumps are specialised, proteinaceous, active-transport mechanisms located in the cytoplasmic membrane of both prokaryotic \(bacterial\) and eukaryotic cells \(Oxford, 2003\). These pumps actively export toxic substances, including multiple classes of antibiotics, from the cytoplasm to the external environment.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
