1618 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 553 0 R
/E (Stand at the edge of the Dover cliffs on a clear morning, and the facade rising 110 metres from the English Channel is visible from the French coast, approximately 32 kilometres away.15 But these are not merely a geological landmark or a national symbol. They are a chemical archive: one hundred million years of marine carbon cycling, compressed into a crystalline sedimentary record and left standing at the edge of a continent. Every square centimetre of this chalk contains the preserved remains of organisms that once dominated the Cretaceous ocean, and every winter storm that breaks against the cliff face enacts a reaction that connects atmospheric carbon dioxide to the dissolution of solid rock.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1619 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 553 0 R
/E (This article traces the chemistry of the White Cliffs from the cellular machinery of ancient algae to the equilibrium equations that govern their modern destruction. The cliffs, it turns out, are a chemistry lecture written in calcium carbonate, and the lesson has not yet finished.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
