/E (Figure 1. The White Cliffs of Dover, rising approximately 110 metres above the English Channel. The cliff face is composed almost entirely of Upper Cretaceous chalk, a biogenic sedimentary rock formed from the compacted remains of trillions of coccolith plates. The distinctive vertical gullies are carved by acidified rainwater dissolving calcium carbonate through the carbonate equilibria described in Section III. Image: Immanuel Giel / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0.)
/Pg 61 0 R
