/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E (I. Introduction)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1829 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E (III. Empathy in males and females)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
1830 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
1831 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E (Additionally, it is evidenced that the primary difference is in levels of emotional empathy, with a self-report study finding women rated themselves as having higher empathetic concern \(Davis, 1983\). )
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
1832 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 566 0 R
/E (Furthermore, biological evidence suggests that different sexes operate using different empathetic systems when approaching situations, with women often showing stronger activation in mirror neuron-related areas, thereby, mirroring the emotions displayed by the person they are empathising towards, i.e. emotional contagion; whereas men are more reliant on regions of cognitive control \(Schulte-Ruther et al, 2008\). This suggests a difference in style; however, provides no conclusive differences in quantity or capacity.)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
