1838 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 567 0 R
/E (Furthermore, it was concluded through experimental and neuropsychological testing measures that no consistent sex effect on empathy; however, the method of assessment was suggested to have great influence on perceived differences in results, with self-reports being vulnerable to unconscious gender stereotypes \(Baez et al, 2017\). This is further strengthened as once expectations were manipulated, differences in self-reported studies disappeared.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1839 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 567 0 R
/E (Therefore, it can be concluded that males and females present their empathy differently, however, in terms of capacity, they possess the same amount. This provides biological evidence that males and females have inherently different systems of empathetic display.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1840 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 567 0 R
/E (It is suggested by modern research that empathy and its display are in part innate \(temperament, brain structure\) and learnt through environment \(parenting, culture, and experiences\).)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1841 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 567 0 R
/E (Biologically, infants possess the basic neural capacities \(systems such as the insula\) for empathy, shown through acts such as crying upon seeing others cry \(emotional contagion\), which is suggested to aid in social bonding \(shared emotionality\) and survival \(pack mentality\), as responsible brain systems are present in primitive humans in addition to other mammals.)
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
