2129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E (This is reflected in the way we conceptualise the idea of mental illness, from speculations of demonic possessions to ailments linked to the condition of the physical body to the complex interaction of internal and external factors around a person; this evolution in psychopathology has allowed humankind to better acknowledge mental disorders. In addition, the worldwide web has fastened the spread of announcements on major events and conflicts, burdening spectators with stress not witnessed during previous eras. )
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
2130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E (These refinements in diagnostic tools and criteria along with increased prevalence due to external stressors are the reasons for the rise in diagnosis, suggesting better recognition.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
2131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E ( However, in nature with modern society, mainstream publication utilising social media has also led to the novelisation of diagnoses, and a profound phenomenon of self-diagnosis. It is plausible then that mental disorder diagnosis may be experiencing modern distortion instead.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E (This demonstrated that the identification and categorisation of mental affliction had existed prior to contemporary science.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E (The late 19th century was shaped by Sigmund Freud, who reasoned that repressed memories and desires were responsible for the behavioural and emotional states, consequently serving as the basis for his psychoanalytic theory along with influencing modern learning theory. )
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 582 0 R
/E (Contradictorily, Emil Kraepelin pioneered a biological approach, which accounted for treatable \(exogenous disorders caused by external factors\) and incurable \(endogenous factors which were products of genetical, neurochemical and structural factors\) mental disorders. )
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
