>>
endobj
2219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 586 0 R
/E ( In an amended DICE model that accounts for rising relative scarcity, Sterner and Persson obtained Stern-like abatement results using Nordhaus's high discount rate. The rate barely mattered once substitutability was correctly specified. What changed my understanding was that specific result. The entire Stern-Nordhaus debate assumes a single composite consumption good. But environmental goods are not consumption goods with green labels. Coral reef collapse has no price. Species extinction is not a depreciating asset. Ice sheet disintegration cannot be reversed by a wealthier civilisation writing a larger cheque. When the damage function is a step function rather than a smooth curve, standard discounting is not merely imprecise. It is structurally wrong.)
/Pg 94 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
2220 0 obj
<<
